# What of our own output is worth keeping in git, and what regenerates.
#
# Figures (.png) ARE tracked - they are the deliverable. Meshes and field dumps are not:
# they are large and a script reproduces them (the ILI mesh rebuilds in ~10 s).
results/**/*.msh
results/**/*.h5
results/**/*.xdmf
results/**/*.npz
results/**/*.bp

# ...except these three. The wide-domain record backs a NULL result and regenerates in
# 12 min; the snapshot runs exist only to make animations and their channel data is a
# byproduct nothing reads.
results/ili_forward/channel_data_*_w165_*.npz
results/ili_forward/channel_data_snap*.npz
# ...but channel_data_snap_p20deg.npz was already tracked when that rule was written,
# and generated data backing the publication is kept. It and the two published pair GIFs
# below are force-tracked (`git add -f`); the rules above only govern NEW files.

# Extracted k-Wave cases: only ~1 min to re-extract, but re-extraction needs data/raw/,
# which is itself gitignored bulk. ~24 MB each.
!results/kwave_cases/*.npz

# Beamformed image caches: tiny, and metric_robustness.py reads them.
!results/compare/images_*.npz

# NEVER track the wavefield snapshot dumps. 476-966 MB each - over GitHub's HARD 100 MB
# per-file limit, so a push carrying one is rejected outright. They rebuild from a ~2 min
# degree-3 GPU solve with --snapshots. Listed after the negations so it wins.
results/**/wavefield_*.npz

# Animations are regenerable and can be enormous - the first wavefield GIF was 64 MB, which
# git warns about and no reviewer wants to clone. Render with viz/wavefield_gif.py
# (--stride / --colors keep them to a few MB) and `git add -f` a small one worth keeping.
results/viz/*.gif

# Generated artifact pages carry ~10-20 MB of inlined base64 images. Rebuild them with
# viz/build_artifacts.py rather than tracking them.
results/artifacts/*.html

# THE RESEARCH TEAM'S RAW RUNS. 423 MB across three runs, read-only for us, and re-obtainable
# from them. tools/extract_kwave_case.py reduces each to a few-MB .npz under results/.
raw/

# GUI run records. One JSON per job, written by the app so a run is reproducible and
# so the gallery can find it. They are a local experiment log, not publication data -
# three of them got tracked and then swept into PUBLICATION_MANIFEST.json, which the
# integrity check correctly failed on the moment a smoke test cleaned them up.
results/gui_runs/
